"Estimates a continuous distribution with a smooth density curve whose area integrates to one.\n\nUse when: Distribution shape is more important than individual observations. A sufficiently large sample supports a transparent smoothing choice.\n\nAvoid when: Discrete values or a very small sample make smoothing misleading.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes the measured value. Curve height encodes estimated density, not the probability at one exact value.\n\nBuild: Choose a kernel bandwidth and disclose its units or selection method. Estimate density over a justified support and respect physical boundaries. Draw the curve with sample size and optional raw observation ticks.\n\nCheck: Check the estimated area is approximately one. Compare plausible bandwidths before claiming multiple modes. Verify the curve does not suggest impossible negative or over-bound values.\n\nPitfalls: Different bandwidths can change the apparent number of clusters. A taller curve indicates concentration, not necessarily a larger sample.\n\nExample: Estimate synthetic response-time density in seconds and include a small rug showing the observed sample.\n\nAlternatives: Histogram, Violin plot, ECDF."
